00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.890
First question is, do
Chromebooks work offline?

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:43.010
That's a great question.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:45.190
I think Chromebooks
have definitely

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:48.910
had a reputation in the past
of being internet-dependent.

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:53.140
Most of our fleet is a
high-memory, high-storage

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.660
device with powerful
processing, which

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.900
means you can actually run
applications in real time

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:03.220
on the Chromebook without
any internet connectivity.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.210
Additionally, you can
work with your files that

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:09.670
are saved locally on
the Chromebook storage

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:12.490
or in your Google
Drive in offline mode.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:14.920
So we have offline
mode for Gmail,

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:18.040
and we also have
offline mode for Drive.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:22.870
You can view and edit Docs,
Sheets, and Slide files

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:24.370
using the offline mode.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:28.600
And it's really as simple as
installing a Chrome extension

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.150
and activating offline mode.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:35.200
I'm a frequent traveler, and
both Gmail and Drive offline

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:39.440
are tools that I leverage
quite frequently.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:40.790
So that's question one.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:42.940
Let's move on to
the second question.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:47.380
Does Microsoft Office
work on a Chromebook?

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.200
Well, we hope you're doing most
of your editing in Google Docs

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.130
formats, but we understand
there may be a one-off need

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.470
to work in a Microsoft format.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:13:01.030
You can save Microsoft native
formats on your Google Drive

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.820
and in local storage
on your Chromebook.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.450
There are really three main ways
to work with Microsoft files

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:11.200
in the Google environment,
on a Chromebook, or in Drive.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.590
The first is through your web
browser, the Chrome browser,

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:16.940
and with a sync client.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:21.580
The second is to install
the Drive sync tool

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.040
in your Microsoft instance.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:29.320
And the third is to upload the
Microsoft file to Google Drive

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:32.950
or on your Chromebook and use
Office Compatibility mode.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:35.590
So you can do all of
your editing in Google

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.180
and move seamlessly
back and forth

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:42.255
between Google format
and Microsoft format.

00:13:42.255 --> 00:13:43.690
[INAUDIBLE] number two.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:44.830
BRANDY AU: Yep.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:46.330
That was great.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:49.000
There's another question
here asking about how

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.220
Chromebooks stay up to date.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.910
So Chromebooks get automatic
updates from Google,

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:57.500
from the Cloud, every six weeks.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:13:59.350
And so it really
takes the onus off

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:03.580
of IT admins to manually
patch and update the devices.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.790
At the same time, IT admins
have complete control

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.220
over how those updates happen.

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:13.990
For example, you can pin some
devices to the current version,

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.630
if there are testing
and validation cycles

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.460
that you have to go through.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.910
Or you can also time the
updates so that they're not

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:26.060
happening when all of your
users are on their devices.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:30.340
So while the updates are
happening in the Cloud

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:32.410
and it takes the
onus off of you,

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:37.000
you can also completely
control how that happens

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.150
And then we have
another question here.

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:43.190
Do you need antivirus
software for Chromebooks?

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:47.480
So there's many layers of
built-in security on a Chrome

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:50.180
device that actually
preclude the need

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.520
for antivirus software.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.450
And so a few examples of
that are, on the app layer,

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.290
all of our apps are read-only,
as well as the extensions,

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:03.910
so that they can't
modify the OS.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:07.040
The sandboxing process we
talked about earlier isolates

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.980
malicious apps and
extensions, and so it really

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:14.120
limit the attack surface
available on a Chromebook.

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:16.880
On the OS level,
there's a concept

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:21.260
called verified boot, which,
whenever the device starts up,

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:25.730
it will always try to detect
whether that version of the OS

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.120
is safe.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.540
And if it's not safe, then
we don't allow the Chromebook

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.720
to go into boot mode.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.790
And then finally,
on a Chromebook,

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:37.420
all of the user data is
encrypted with our hardware

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:38.540
security module.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:40.390
And that's built into
every Chrome device

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:43.120
that we manufacture.

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:47.230
And because of the fact that
users can't install executables

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.350
on a Chrome device, that
really reduces the chances

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:53.485
of malicious software getting
installed onto your device.

00:15:55.762 --> 00:15:56.970
CHRISTOPHER JONES: All right.

